# /etc/usb-hotplug/pools.conf — USB pool mappings
#
# Pools solve the "two identical USB devices" problem that standard PVE
# mappings can't: when you have N copies of the same VID:PID with no unique
# iSerial (game controllers, WiFi adapters, DACs, etc.), there's no way to
# tell them apart. A pool attaches whichever devices are present, by live
# bus-port, into the lowest free usbN slots on a target VM.
#
# Format — one pool per non-comment line, whitespace-separated:
#
#   <pool_name>  <vid:pid>  <target_vmid>  <max_devices>  [usb3]
#
# Fields:
#   pool_name    free-form, used in logs only
#   vid:pid      4-hex:4-hex (lowercase), the USB VID:PID of the device class
#   target_vmid  the VM the devices should attach to (must be on this node);
#                or "shared"/"gpu" → the current GPU VM (follows whichever GPU
#                VM is running, like shared-* mappings)
#   max_devices  the upper bound on number of devices to attach simultaneously
#                — extras beyond this count are ignored
#   usb3         optional last field — pass "usb3" or "1" to force usb3=1 on
#                attach. Omit for default (USB2 emulation).
#
# Legacy 6-field lines (<name> <vid:pid> <target> <first_slot> <max> [usb3])
# still parse; first_slot no longer reserves slots.
#
# Behavior:
#   - Pool fires every poll alongside the standard mapping logic.
#   - Each device attaches by its live bus-port (host=<port>) into the lowest
#     FREE usbN slot (same rule as shared-*/vmN-* mappings — no reserved range,
#     so pools and mappings never collide).
#   - Sticky: a device keeps its slot while plugged in. On unplug only its own
#     slot is deleted; other pool devices never move.
#   - The pool only removes host= slots it attached itself. mapping=... slots
#     and manual host= slots for other devices are never touched.
#   - Pool entries targeting cross-node VMs are silently skipped.

# Example: 2× SteelSeries Stratus Duo dongles (1038:1430) → current GPU VM, USB3 enabled
# steelseries  1038:1430  shared  2  usb3

# Example: Alfa AWUS036NH USB wifi (148f:3070) → this host's VM 200 router
# alfa-router  148f:3070  200  1
